◊*“*®1∞x—@ÄAÿè8â8‘+®A∞f”=”>–>rU␀␀␀⁄rb)⁄modec␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Ûº␀␀␀ï␀/␀nU␀␀H?␀␀nUR␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀5␀␀␀␀␀␀␀n[␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀U5␀␀␀␀␀␀(␀␀␀␀␀␀␀d␀␀M%␀␀UR␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀[␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀U5␀␀␀␀␀␀5␀␀␀␀␀␀ ␀MA␀␀␀ ␀[␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀U5␀␀␀␀␀␀S:X␀␀a␀␀US␀␀␀$␀U$␀)a¶␀␀Read an undirected graph in sparse6 format from path.

Parameters
----------
path : file or string
   Filename or file handle to read.
   Filenames ending in .gz or .bz2 will be decompressed.

Returns
-------
G : Graph/Multigraph or list of Graphs/MultiGraphs
   If the file contains multiple lines then a list of graphs is returned

Raises
------
NetworkXError
    If the string is unable to be parsed in sparse6 format

Examples
--------
You can read a sparse6 file by giving the path to the file::

    >>> import tempfile
    >>> with tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile(delete=False) as f:
    ...     _ = f.write(b">>sparse6<<:An\n")
    ...     _ = f.seek(0)
    ...     G = nx.read_sparse6(f.name)
    >>> list(G.edges())
    [(0, 1)]

You can also read a sparse6 file by giving an open file-like object::

    >>> import tempfile
    >>> with tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile() as f:
    ...     _ = f.write(b">>sparse6<<:An\n")
    ...     _ = f.seek(0)
    ...     G = nx.read_sparse6(f)
    >>> list(G.edges())
    [(0, 1)]

See Also
--------
read_sparse6, from_sparse6_bytes

References
----------
.. [1] Sparse6 specification
       <https://users.cecs.anu.edu.au/~bdm/data/formats.html>

